Well....

The longest gap I'm aware of was after 3.1.3, there were several years
between that and 3.2.0, possibly having to do with Izchak's death (may
all prayers and blessings be upon him!).  3.1, changed a lot of things
about the game, that was where we got the planes and Astral from, the
Wizard's tower, the backstory, the deepest level, priests of Moloch, I
think Quests too, and the three items needed to get the Amulet, among
other things.

Since then there's been changes, but nothing really on that scale.
Whether we'll ever see something of that scale again, I don't know.
Slash'em has the whole keys of law/neutrality/chaos thing and a slew of
new levels, but other than that the basic structure of the quest is
still the same.

I like the idea of the DevTeam serving in a kind of editorial capacity,
as an arbiter and compiler of community-supplied patches.  Whether they
see themselves in that light I couldn't say, but a good number of
features got their start in user-created patches, or Nethack variants.
The lessons of open source software seem to imply that this kind of
thing should work, and indeed it is obvious that Nethack has benefited
from it to some degree.

Concerning those patches: while official Nethack releases are few and
far between these days, we're living in a kind of golden period of user
patch activity.  There are patches that add new classes, large numbers
of new monsters, new objects, new game systems (color alchemy, wow!),
new levels and branches, new game mechanics (like pet rebellion), new
room types (I myself made a couple of those), and new aspects to the
game like the Grudge Patch.  And of course, there's also Slash'em,
which adds much that I don't personally agree with, but there's also a
lot of coolness there (like the Guild of Disgruntled Adventurers).

Indeed, there are so many patches out there now that someone with some
ability in C could roll their own variant, and indeed PatchHack looked
very much like that.

Hmm, maybe this needs its own thread....

